    "long_instructions": "# Ethical Data Collection \r\n\r\n## 📝 To Do\r\n\r\n#### In this activity, students will read the analyze three different scenarios, each about a different concept and context: privacy regarding food allergies, misuse & consent regarding sponsorships, and accuracy and bias regarding entertainment.\r\n\r\n<img src=\"https://images.code.org/9145933afbe673b1f8165dab633a1ed2-image-1724339822547.png\" alt=\"pryamid of ethical concerns of data - misuse and consent, privacy, and accuracy and bias\" width=\"600px\" style=\"display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;\">\r\n\r\n#### *Here is one of the three scenarios from the lesson.*\r\n\r\n#### Read the student conversations presented in the scenario and compose a statement at provides guidance about ethical data collection based on the conversation between classmates.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<table style=\"width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; border: 2px solid black;\">\r\n <tr>\r\n    <td colspan=\"3\" style=\"border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">\r\n      <h1 style=\"text-align: center;\">Party Planning: Food Allergies</h1>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td style=\"text-align: center; border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://images.code.org/f2eed9e0ea3d6831513593aa4e22715e-image-1718126411527.png\" alt=\"First Image\" width=\"100\"></td>\r\n    <td style=\"text-align: center; border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://images.code.org/1bbc85a2d6f1a8e451bef3b1eb8ab7da-image-1718127335468.png\" alt=\"Second Image\" width=\"100\"></td>\r\n    <td style=\"text-align: center; border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\"><img src=\"https://images.code.org/994e6bda34788751fef656f2332e595f-image-1718127355721.png\" alt=\"Third Image\" width=\"100\"></td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n  <tr>\r\n    <td style=\"text-align: center; border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">\r\n      <h2>Desiree</h2>\r\n      <p style=\"text-align: left;\">“I think that we need to account for student food allergies at our party! I know that I am allergic to pineapple– which some people love on their pizza for some reason. We should send around a survey and collect student names, grade, ID numbers, and the different allergies that they have. That way we can accommodate everyone!”</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td style=\"text-align: center; border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">\r\n      <h2>Tabor</h2>\r\n      <p style=\"text-align: left;\">“Totally get where you’re coming from with that. I worry that having student ID numbers might be little bit too much information, though. Like our usernames on the school network and our lunch PINs rely on that number. I think that might be a little too strange for people to be giving that up.”</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n    <td style=\"text-align: center; border: 1px solid black; padding: 10px;\">\r\n      <h2>Julia</h2>\r\n      <p style=\"text-align: left;\">“Here’s the thing- I have food allergies, too, so I get the importance of collecting the information, Desiree. What I’m not comfortable with, however, is filling out a survey that links me to my personal medical history in our data collection. I don’t think that we should be asking students for that information.”</p>\r\n    </td>\r\n  </tr>\r\n</table>\r\n\r\n#### As your students work through the scenarios, they should pay attention to how data is collected, whether through surveys, automated methods, or measurements.\r\n\r\n#### This activity will naturally lead to discussions about ethical concerns related to data collection, such as privacy issues, misuse of data, accuracy, and bias.\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<div style=\"background-color: #a6e3e8; border-radius: 40px; color: #000000; width: 95%; padding: 1px 20px 1px 20px;\">\r\n\r\n<p style=\"line-height: 1.5; margin-left: 10px; margin-right: 10px; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 18px; \"><strong>Teacher Tip:</strong> Encourage your students to think of their classifications as flexible hypotheses rather than fixed answers. Highlight how ethical data collection often requires iterative thinking—just like they're doing in this activity. Draw parallels to real-world examples, like how organizations reassess their data collection practices when bias or ethical concerns are identified. \r\n</p>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n<br>\r\n\r\n<p style=\"font-size: 18px; font-weight: bold; background-color: #F5F7F8; padding: 10px; border-left: 5px solid #8148B2; display: inline-block;\">\r\n    ➡️ This activity is from Lesson 3. You can find more details in the full <a href='https://studio.code.org/courses/artificial-intelligence-foundations-v2-2025/units/6/lessons/3' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer' style='text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold;'>lesson plan</a>.\r\n</p> ",
